#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102005 :tutorial/content "The graph of $y=f'(x),$ the derivative of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Let's use this to sketch a possible graph of the original function, $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/6c/6cd6c0fa999d571b54b7408856ecc8d091c2f4bb16b9f7bf85eb7f91b05629e4.png)\n\nRecall that the critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there are two critical points: \n\n$$\nx=-3\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <         0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical points. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical lines $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1),$ the point at $x=1$ must be lower than the point at $x=-3,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb0de4712344b43d32343f4d9fbc11d3dbc5c96df858f27bee599a3ffdd448ce.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-3)$ and decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes down to the left and to the right of $x=-3.$\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $\\searrow$ on $(-3,1)$ and increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(1,\\infty)$, the graph of $f(x)$ goes up to the left and to the right of $x=1.$\n\n![](images/34/3498651b6a642f59068f23e8ed9ddd211bfb76ab4d0bf8e28cefd84d1a4b2aed.png)\n\n- Finally, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/25/257203aa25e423bf82ce745a6a8a3563aa80e414e9dfadf0be64629624cbce0e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0173ff8c-84c4-5761-9aad-f4e4362d1e7f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 651 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102006 :tutorial/content "Let's draw our possible function $y=f(x)$ once more. The graph of its derivative $y=f'(x)$ is also shown.\n\n![](images/18/189c181d3767960f39fc0cfcb06dac5bf6fca07e303b11999307551d081ffc1e.png)\n\nIn reality, there are infinitely many possible graphs of $f(x)$ that can be drawn if we are given only the graph of $f'(x).$ We can always shift the graph up and down as we please, because the slopes of the tangent lines do not change. For the problem above, any of the following graphs would be valid:\n\n![](images/e4/e49f65d7306192d73001552084f06cf39731f38f3ccc14dd6b2f5f7641ec43aa.png)\n\nIn this topic, we will focus on sketching a *possible* graph of $y=f(x)$ given the graph of $y=f'(x).$ But bear in mind that whatever $y=f(x)$ we sketch, we can get an equally valid answer by shifting or $y=f(x)$ curve up or down by an arbitrary amount. \n\nThe only exception is if we're given a point on the curve $y=f(x).$ If we're given a point, this has the effect of *fixing* our answer to a particular curve. We'll see an example of this next." :tutorial/id #uuid "aed3a1d0-c855-5799-a9a4-76d3c17c10bf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 653 :tutorial/title "Non-Uniqueness in Sketching a Function Given the Graph of Its Derivative"}]}
